readme.migration -- Converting an old configuration
===================================================

Earlier versions of Services read two line-based files, ircservices.conf
and modules.conf, with their own parser.  The configuration is now a single
block-structured file (ircservices.conf, which may include others) read by
a bison/flex parser; see readme.config.  Old files are not accepted: the
first old-style line is reported as a syntax error.  This file explains how
to convert them.

What changed
------------

- One configuration: ircservices.conf is the only file Services open.
  modules.conf is gone; the module settings go in ircservices.conf
  itself (or, if you prefer, in a file it includes).
- Blocks and semicolons: core settings are grouped in blocks
  (serverinfo, uplink, files, options, timeouts, netbuffer) with new,
  lower-case names; module settings go in `module "<name>" { ... };`
  blocks and keep their names.
- Every entry ends with `;`, and several values are separated by commas.
- `IncludeFile` became `include`, `LoadModule` became `loadmodule`,
  `LoadLanguageText` became `loadlanguage`.
- Flags may be written with yes/no.
- The whole configuration is parsed before any module is loaded, and on
  REHASH a configuration with errors is rejected as a whole.

Step 1: the core settings
-------------------------

Move each directive of the old ircservices.conf to its new place (the full
table is in readme.config, section 4-8):

    Old                                   New
    ------------------------------------  ---------------------------------
    RemoteServer 127.0.0.1 4400 "pass"    uplink {
    LocalAddress 192.0.2.10                   host = 127.0.0.1;
                                              port = 4400;
                                              password = "pass";
                                              local_address = 192.0.2.10;
                                          };
    ServerName "services.example.net"     serverinfo {
    ServerDesc "Services"                     name = "services.example.net";
    ServerNumeric 4094                        description = "Services";
    ServiceUser "services@example.net"        numeric = 4094;
                                              user = "services@example.net";
                                          };
    LogFilename ircservices.log           files {
    PIDFilename ircservices.pid               log = "ircservices.log";
    MOTDFilename ircservices.motd             pid = "ircservices.pid";
    LockFilename .lock                        motd = "ircservices.motd";
    Umask 077                                 umask = 077;
    RunGroup =65534                           run_group = "=65534";
                                          };
    GuestNickPrefix "Guest"               options {
    ListMax 50                                guest_nick_prefix = "Guest";
    StrictPasswords                           list_max = 50;
    BadPassLimit 5                            strict_passwords;
    RejectEmail *@example.com                 bad_pass_limit = 5;
    RejectEmail *@example.org                 reject_email = "*@example.com",
    DefTimeZone PST8PDT                                      "*@example.org";
                                              timezone = PST8PDT;
                                          };
    UpdateTimeout 5m                      timeouts {
    WarningTimeout 4h                         update = 5m;
    ReadTimeout 3                             warning = 4h;
    TimeoutCheck 1.0                          read = 3;
                                              check = 1.0;
                                          };
    NetBufferSize 4194304 1048576         netbuffer {
    NetBufferLimit 80 95                      size = 4194304, 1048576;
                                              limit = 80, 95;
                                          };
    LoadModule nickserv/main              loadmodule nickserv/main;
    LoadLanguageText mytext.l             loadlanguage "mytext.l";
    IncludeFile "local.conf"              include "local.conf";

ExpireTimeout, which had no effect, no longer exists.

Step 2: the module settings
---------------------------

The module settings keep their names; only the syntax changes.  For each
`Module ... EndModule` section of the old modules.conf:

1. `Module <name>` becomes `module "<name>" {` and `EndModule` becomes
   `};`.
2. Each directive `Name value value ...` becomes `Name = value, value, ...;`.
3. Each directive without parameters (a flag) `Name` becomes `Name;`.
4. Values that contain blanks or any of `{ } ; = , " #` must be quoted.
   Watch in particular the alias settings, whose value contains `=`:

       NSAlias ID=IDENTIFY          ->  NSAlias = "ID=IDENTIFY";

For example:

    Module nickserv/main                 module "nickserv/main" {
        NickServName "NickServ" "Nick"       NickServName = "NickServ", "Nick";
        NSEnableRegister                     NSEnableRegister;
        NSRegDelay 5m                        NSRegDelay = 5m;
        NSAlias ID=IDENTIFY                  NSAlias = "ID=IDENTIFY";
    EndModule                            };

Paste the converted blocks into ircservices.conf, after the loadmodule
entries (the example keeps them at the end, under "Module settings").  If
you would rather keep them in a separate file, add to ircservices.conf

    include "modules.conf";

but nothing requires it.  Every loadmodule entry
must stay in the file: a module block does not load its module.

Step 3: check
-------------

Start Services in the foreground:

    bin/ircservices -nofork -debug

Every problem is reported with its file and line; the most common ones
after a conversion are a missing `;` (reported on the next line), an
unquoted value containing `=` or `#`, and a core directive left under its
old name (reported as an unknown directive, or as a syntax error if it has
no `;`).  With -debug, the log also lists module blocks for modules that
are not loaded, which catches a misspelt module name.

The example file data/example-ircservices.conf contains every setting,
core and module, in the new syntax, with the same documentation as
before.

The databases
-------------

The file databases are gone too: Services keep their data in PostgreSQL
(readme.database), and the database/standard and database/version4
modules, their module blocks and their loadmodule entries no longer exist
(remove them; a leftover loadmodule entry stops Services from starting).
LockFilename (files { lock; }) and the -forceload option went with them.

Add a database block with the connection string and a redis block (both
are required), and bring the data over with the XML export and import
modules: export with the old version (-export=<file>), then start this one
once with -import=<file>.  readme.database, section 1-7, has the details.

